# The Event Sourcing Paradigm

At this point we have a barebones Phoenix project, which has a room schema and CRUD pages.  A room has an id, name and description.  The Phoenix generators created some basic CRUD pages which allow us to create, update and delete rooms using the HTML forms provided.  Now what?

Let's take a step back and examine the hard problem we're trying solve.  Each client/player (I will use client and player interchangeably) will visit the "show" path for the room `/rooms/:id` and expect to "see" something.  They will also expect to "see" each other.  And when players manipulate the environment (pick up and throw an object, kill a monster, open a door, pickup a key), we expect other players to see those changes in their own respective browsers.  So the big problem definition we have is a syncronization problem between the clients.  How do we provide all clients with the current state of the world, take input changes from all clients, and syncronize those changes back out to each client?

This sounds like a job for event sourcing.

Event sourcing is simply recording all events as facts in a cronological ledger.  This historical record of all the facts is a "stream" of data called the event stream.  All clients are constantly producing events, just by entering the room and moving around.  So all clients are event producers.  From the event stream we can build "projections" or alternate representations of the state of the world.  In fact, each client is also consumer of all events that all clients have created and will create a projection of the state of the world.  Each client can be thought of as rendering an independent simulation of their world and the only thing keeping all the clients in sync is the data that is streamed to them.  

## Create the Events table

Let's begin by building a database table that will serve as a journal for all the events that happen in a single room.  The table will need a uuid for each event, a sequence id to order the event, timestamps, a room_id to associate all the events belonging to a room, an event name and a payload for the event.

This generator will create an events table and add it under the rooms context.  It will also create some basic HTML CRUD pages, but we won't end up using most of them.

```bash
mix phx.gen.html Rooms Event events room_id:references:rooms sequence:integer event_name:string payload:map --binary-id
```

Make the following changes to the migration file:

```elixir
defmodule Xr.Repo.Migrations.CreateEvents do
  use Ecto.Migration

  def change do
    create table(:events, primary_key: false) do
      add :id, :binary_id, primary_key: true
      add :sequence, :integer, null: false, default: 0
      add :event_name, :string, null: false
      add :payload, :map, default: %{}
      add :room_id, references(:rooms, on_delete: :delete_all, type: :string)

      timestamps(type: :utc_datetime)
    end

    create index(:events, [:room_id])
  end
end

```

Run the migration:

```bash
mix ecto.migrate
```

