## Create some events every time the room is created


The generator added some functions to the rooms.ex context file.  We can remove those and replace with just this:

```elixir
  alias Xr.Rooms.Event

  @doc """
  Returns the list of events.

  ## Examples

      iex> list_events()
      [%Event{}, ...]

  """
  def list_room_events(room_id) do
    Repo.all(from e in Event, where: e.room_id == ^room_id, order_by: e.sequence)
  end

  @doc """
  Creates a event.

  ## Examples

      iex> create_event(%{field: value})
      {:ok, %Event{}}

      iex> create_event(%{field: bad_value})
      {:error, %Ecto.Changeset{}}

  """
  def create_event(attrs \\ %{}) do
    %Event{}
    |> Event.changeset(attrs)
    |> Repo.insert()
  end
  ```

  When we list events it only makes sense to do so in the context of a room, so we need a list_room_events function that takes a room_id.  Next we can get rid of any update or delete event functions because events will be immutable and never change.  They are facts that happened at a point in time.

  Let's also remove all the event tests from the rooms test file and fix the function name in the EventController.

```elixir
defmodule XrWeb.EventController do
  use XrWeb, :controller

  alias Xr.Rooms

  def index(conn, %{"room_id" => room_id}) do
    events = Rooms.list_room_events(room_id)
    render(conn, :index, events: events)
  end
end

```



Our room doesn't look 3D yet.  It's just a record in a table now.  To render a 3D scene, we need some things to render.  Suppose different rooms contained different objects like different colored boxes.  Then when we go to the "show" path `/rooms/:id`, instead of showing the room name and description, we can render a Babylon.js 3D scene with all the objects in the room.  But we'll need to store different content for each room, so we'll need a table for that.

